  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 352 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nSunday, a strong 597-dam 500-mb ridge will build and amplify across \nthe central United States, while a longwave trough remains \nestablished across the western United States. This will maintain \nweak west to southwest flow aloft across the region. Warm air \nadvection will persist through the afternoon, with 850-mb \ntemperatures rising into the 25 to 30C range, which is quite warm \nfor this time of year. Deep boundary-layer mixing should support \nhigh temperatures in the lower to middle 90s across much of the \narea. A weak disturbance is forecast to move into western Nebraska \nby late Sunday afternoon into the evening, which may provide enough \nforcing for isolated showers and thunderstorm development. However, \nweak large-scale forcing and limited deep-layer shear should keep \nthe severe weather threat low. Some fire weather concerns may \ndevelop Sunday afternoon across western and southwest Nebraska as \nminimum relative humidity values approach 25 percent and southerly \nwinds become gusty. Overnight lows will remain mild, generally in \nthe middle to upper 60s.\n\nMonday, the strong upper-level ridge will remain centered across the \nsouth-central United States while a shortwave ejects northeastward \nalong its western periphery from Wyoming into the Dakotas. \nSouthwesterly flow aloft will strengthen across the region as this \ndisturbance approaches, resulting in an increasingly favorable \nkinematic environment. Monday afternoon and evening currently appear \nmore favorable for severe weather compared to Sunday. Ample \ninstability combined with increasing deep-layer shear may support \norganized convection. As mentioned in the previous discussion, a \nfrontal boundary is forecast to move into the area and should \nprovide a focus for more widespread shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment. The eventual position and timing of this boundary, \nalong with the magnitude of instability that develops ahead of it, \nwill play an important role in determining the severe weather \nthreat. This period will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles. Highs Monday will remain in the lower to \nmiddle 90s, with overnight lows generally in the middle to upper 60s.\n\nTuesday through late next week, temperatures should moderate \nsomewhat behind Mondays frontal passage, with highs generally in the \nmiddle to upper 80s. The upper-level pattern is forecast to become \nmore zonal with periodic weak disturbances moving through the \nregion. This should maintain at least scattered precipitation \nchances through the end of the forecast period, though confidence in \nthe timing and coverage of individual rounds remains limited at this \nrange. Overall, confidence remains high in continued warm \ntemperatures, with moderate confidence in periodic precipitation \nchances through late next week.",
